---
import ProseLayout from '@/layouts/ProseLayout.astro';
import EvidenceTag from '@/components/EvidenceTag.astro';
---

<ProseLayout
  eyebrow="Methodology"
  title="How claims are sourced and labeled"
  description="How Runtime Signals sources, labels, and reviews claims before publication."
  canonicalPath="/methodology"
  traceSeed={5}
>
  <p>
    Every article names a real system problem, states the invariant it violates, explains the
    failure mechanism, and gives an implementable practice - with limitations stated, not implied.
    Every consequential claim gets one of four labels, shown on the article itself:
  </p>
  <ul class="evidence-key">
    <li><EvidenceTag evidence="supported" /> - backed by a primary source, cited.</li>
    <li><EvidenceTag evidence="mixed" /> - partially supported; the gap is stated.</li>
    <li>
      <EvidenceTag evidence="inference" /> - our reasoning from available evidence, not a direct source
      claim.
    </li>
    <li><EvidenceTag evidence="opinion" /> - an editorial judgment, labeled as one.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A claim labeled <EvidenceTag evidence="supported" /> or <EvidenceTag evidence="mixed" /> is rejected
    at the publication gate if it cites no source - this is enforced in the content schema, not just editorial
    review.
  </p>

  <h2>Source priority</h2>
  <p>Ranked, not arbitrary - each tier below only backs a claim when nothing higher applies.</p>
  <ol class="priority-list">
    <li>
      Standards, specifications, official documentation, source repositories, and original papers.
    </li>
    <li>Maintainer-authored engineering reports and reproducible production write-ups.</li>
    <li>Benchmark and dataset documentation and code.</li>
    <li>
      Credible secondary analysis, for discovery or context - never as sole proof for a
      consequential claim.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Every source record keeps its access date, since pricing, limits, APIs, and policies change. We
    recheck time-sensitive claims before publishing and before any claim likely to have moved.
  </p>
</ProseLayout>

<style>
  .evidence-key {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-3);
    margin: var(--space-5) 0;
  }

  /* Ranked tiers, not a generic bullet list - a lower tier only applies when
     every tier above it doesn't, so the numbering itself is the information
     (the same "structure is information" reasoning as the editorial-policy
     workflow trace, applied to a plain ordered list here since a ranked
     priority doesn't need its own bespoke visual device to read as ranked). */
  .priority-list {
    padding-left: 0;
    margin: var(--space-5) 0;
    list-style: none;
    counter-reset: priority;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-3);
  }

  .priority-list li {
    counter-increment: priority;
    padding-left: var(--space-6);
    position: relative;
  }

  .priority-list li::before {
    content: counter(priority);
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--signal);
    border: 1px solid var(--signal);
    border-radius: 50%;
    width: 1.25rem;
    height: 1.25rem;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
  }
</style>
